WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.075 --> 00:00:02.227
- We just hired our first
chief creative officer.

00:00:02.227 --> 00:00:05.317
I spent six months trying to
find the person and this was the

00:00:05.317 --> 00:00:08.675
order of what mattered to me:
first, emotional intelligence.

00:00:08.675 --> 00:00:10.408
When I tell you that I don't

00:00:10.408 --> 00:00:12.740
respect talent, 
it would upset you.

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:16.779
It's stunning when I run my
business how secondary pure

00:00:16.779 --> 00:00:18.985
talent is to people skills.

00:00:18.985 --> 00:00:23.965
This notion that some creative
is so fucking special that they

00:00:23.965 --> 00:00:26.542
can be a douche bag and fuck
up the entire place is the

00:00:26.542 --> 00:00:29.080
stupidest shit I've
ever heard in my life.

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:32.183
And I'm very unpopular in
tech-land, I did it again at

00:00:32.183 --> 00:00:34.065
South-By where I was
like, "Fuck Steve Jobs."

00:00:34.065 --> 00:00:37.397
I don't want to run a business
where being mean brings

00:00:37.397 --> 00:00:38.992
the best out of.

00:00:38.992 --> 00:00:40.691
I don't give a shit if
you're a creative genius.

00:00:40.691 --> 00:00:42.822
Fucking act like a
normal person, you asshole.

00:00:42.822 --> 00:00:43.947
(laughter)

00:00:43.947 --> 00:00:46.420
I believe continuity 
trumps everything.

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:50.074
Continuity, you know, it's just
like sports. A team that stays

00:00:50.074 --> 00:00:52.369
together usually beats a team
with superstars that were put

00:00:52.369 --> 00:00:53.968
together for one season.

00:00:53.968 --> 00:00:55.188
And I believe that.

00:00:55.188 --> 00:00:57.114
And I love continuity at Vayner.

00:00:57.114 --> 00:00:58.936
We have enormous continuity
already for a young company and

00:00:58.936 --> 00:01:01.294
I want to keep it
forever because I give a shit.

00:01:01.294 --> 00:01:03.903
Rhys is now living in London,
she's going to be part of the

00:01:03.903 --> 00:01:05.951
London team because four years
ago when she started working for

00:01:05.951 --> 00:01:07.102
me I'm like,
"What do you want?"

00:01:07.102 --> 00:01:07.924
She's like "I want
to be in London."

00:01:07.924 --> 00:01:08.926
I'm like, "Cool, if we ever go
there, you'll go there,"

00:01:08.926 --> 00:01:11.075
and then I did it.

00:01:11.075 --> 00:01:14.960
And by the way, I don't care
if you're driven by money or by

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:19.450
work-life balance or by creative
output I individually at 650

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:22.515
care about what you care about
and I also know when you get

00:01:22.515 --> 00:01:24.986
married you're going to
care about different shit.

00:01:24.986 --> 00:01:26.637
We're an HR driven organization.

00:01:26.637 --> 00:01:28.298
I needed to find a CCO that

00:01:28.298 --> 00:01:31.489
understood that so 
that was a challenge.

00:01:31.489 --> 00:01:34.007
Especially when you're looking
for them and I was looking for

00:01:34.007 --> 00:01:37.996
somebody from a world like this
because I wanted to taste what a

00:01:37.996 --> 00:01:43.262
seasoned veteran from a Widen,
from a Crispin looked like.

00:01:43.262 --> 00:01:45.613
What did that feel
like internally?

00:01:45.613 --> 00:01:47.472
(light music)

